<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The New John Brown Song (William Weston Patton)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic (Julia Ward Howe)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune; written November 1861, published February 1862</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gen. Halleck's Grand March (Septimus Winner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1862</span><span class="sg-chain-h">theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The President's Proclamation (Edna Dean Proctor)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the First of Arkansas (Lindley Miller)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1864</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Valiant Soldiers (attributed to Sojourner Truth)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kriegslied der Division Blenker</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new German words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marching song of the 4th Battalion of Rifles, 13th Regiment, Massachusetts Volunteers</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Königgrätz March (Johann Gottfried Piefke)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1866</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Battle Hymn of Cooperation</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bummers, Come and Meet Us</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><a href="/songs/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune; anthem of the Industrial Workers of the World</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Blood on the Risers</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune (World War II American paratroopers)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Capstan shanty</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as a sea shanty</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Burning of the School</span><span class="sg-chain-h">children's parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Alle Kinder lernen lesen</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German children's song, same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Badluram Ka Badan (Assam Regiment)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, modified refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Glory Glory</span><span class="sg-chain-h">football chant</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Canti della libertà (Milva, Italian words by Gino Negri)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1965</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">John Brown / Cavalca cowboy (Los Marcellos Ferial, words by Marcello Minerbi and Tullio Romano)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Italian words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Move on over (Len Chandler)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Say, Brothers, Will You Meet Us (camp-meeting hymn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1806</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune and the hallelujah chorus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Say, Bummers, Will You Meet Us (William Steffe, by his account)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1855</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a variant of the same tune</span></li></ul></section>
